{"title":"Lantern Vaccination Centre","sub":"From 120 to 200 people an hour","type":"Public sector","industry":"Public health","geo":"Canada","diff":3,"fmt":"Candidate-led","time":"25-30 min","skills":"Process throughput · moving bottlenecks · public services","prompt":"A city health department runs a mass vaccination centre in a sports hall during a flu-and-COVID campaign. It vaccinates about 120 people an hour; the target is 200. The centre manager wants to hire more nurses. The deputy health officer asks you how to reach 200 an hour at the lowest cost.","clarify":[{"q":"Process?","a":"Check-in desk, then a screening interview (health questions, consent), then vaccination, then 15 minutes' observation in a chair."},{"q":"Staff and times?","a":"Check-in: 4 desks, 1.5 minutes each. Screening: 6 nurses, 3 minutes each. Vaccination: 8 vaccinators, 2 minutes each. Observation: 60 chairs."},{"q":"Online forms?","a":"If people complete health questions online before they arrive, screening takes about 1 minute. About 70% would do so if asked when booking."}],"structure":{"root":"How can the centre reach 200 people an hour?","branches":[{"head":"Capacity per station","items":["Staff × 60 ÷ minutes each","Chairs ÷ observation time"]},{"head":"Bottleneck","items":["Slowest station today","Next bottleneck after each fix"]},{"head":"Cheapest fixes","items":["Remove work vs add staff"]}]},"hypothesis":"Throughput is set by the slowest step. Screening looks slowest; but after fixing it, another step will probably limit the flow.","questions":[{"q":"Work out the capacity of each station per hour. Where is the bottleneck?","exhibit":{"kind":"chart","svg":"<svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"400.043pt\" height=\"163.444pt\" viewBox=\"0 0 400.043 163.444\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" version=\"1.1\"><defs><style type=\"text/css\">*{stroke-linejoin: round; stroke-linecap: butt}</style></defs><g id=\"figure_1\"><g id=\"patch_1\"><path d=\"M 0 163.444 L 4